      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the largest 4-digit integer that is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), we need to determine the largest 4-digit integer \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[\nn \\equiv 15 \\pmod{22}\n\\]\n\nThis means that when \\( n \\) is divided by 22, the remainder is 15. In other words, \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\n\\[\nn = 22k + 15\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer.\n\nTo find the largest 4-digit integer satisfying this condition, we need:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15 \\leq 9999\n\\]\n\nFirst, solve for the lower bound:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\leq 22k + 15\n\\]\n\nSubtract 15 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n985 \\leq 22k\n\\]\n\nDivide by 22:\n\n\\[\nk \\geq \\frac{985}{22} \\approx 44.7727\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k \\) must be an integer, we take the smallest integer greater than or equal to 44.7727, which is:\n\n\\[\nk = 45\n\\]\n\nSo, when \\( k = 45 \\):\n\n\\[\nn = 22 \\times 45 + 15 = 990 + 15 = 1005\n\\]\n\nNow, check if \\( n = 1005 \\) is indeed greater than or equal to 1000. Since \\( 1005 \\) is indeed a 4-digit number and is congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\), it is the largest 4-digit integer that satisfies the condition.\n\nTherefore, the largest 4-digit integer congruent to \\( 15 \\pmod{22} \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1005}\n\\]",
